%U A371956 107869952,239539786,461697822,1017586166,1967029410,4325859600,8368093759,18327074703,35563210285,77461297027,150637173522
%N A371956 Number of non-biquanimous compositions of 2n.
%C A371956 A finite multiset of numbers is defined to be biquanimous iff it can be partitioned into two multisets with equal sums. Biquanimous partitions are counted by A002219 and ranked by A357976.
%C A371956 Biquanimity depends only on the multiset of parts, not on their order, so a(n) can be found by testing each partition of 2n once (for a sub-multiset summing to n) and weighting it by its number of distinct arrangements; this avoids enumerating all 2^(2n-1) compositions. Building the partition with parts in descending order while carrying the set of reachable sub-sums lets the search be cut as soon as n becomes reachable, since reachability only grows as parts are added; what survives is essentially A006827(n) leaves rather than p(2n). - _Alper Ferudun_, Jul 29 2026
%H A371956 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A371956/b371956.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..100</a>
%H A371956 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A371956/a371956.txt">C++ program</a>
%e A371956 The a(1) = 1 through a(3) = 9 compositions:
%e A371956   (2)  (4)    (6)
%e A371956        (1,3)  (1,5)
%e A371956        (3,1)  (2,4)
%e A371956               (4,2)
